verdicts.evidence:
`start-wolfchatter` is six lines and line 6 is `exec node scripts/start/main.ts "$@"` — the entry point is a `.ts` file, and the repo's root `package.json` has `"type": "module"`, so Node resolves it through the ESM loader, which derives format from the extension. Any runtime without native type-stripping throws in `node:internal/modules/esm/get_format` before a single line of `main.ts` is evaluated. I reproduced exactly that by running Node 24.20 with stripping switched off:

  $ node --no-experimental-strip-types scripts/start/main.ts --yes
  TypeError [ERR_UNKNOWN_FILE_EXTENSION]: Unknown file extension ".ts" for /Users/raduniculae/code/wolfchatter/scripts/start/main.ts
      at Object.getFileProtocolModuleFormat [as file:] (node:internal/modules/esm/get_format:243:9)

No "Wolfchatter needs Node 24.16 or newer" line is printed. The guard is `main.ts:68-71`:

  if (!isSupportedNodeVersion(process.version)) {
    console.error(`Wolfchatter needs Node 24.16 or newer; this is ${process.version}.`);
    process.exit(1);
  }

It sits below the import block, so it is reachable only on runtimes that could already load the file — Node 22.18+, 23.6+ and 24.0-24.15, where stripping is unflagged. For Node 22.17 and older, and every Node 20 and below, the branch is dead and the user gets a bare stack trace naming nothing about versions. The audience is the documented one: README "Running it" says "Docker is the only prerequisite" and never asks for a Node version, and PRD §"First run" states the wizard "checks the Node version" — architecture.md §11 step 1 promises "Node ≥ 24.16 (otherwise print the `nvm install` line from `.nvmrc` and stop)". Two smaller facts fall out of the same read and support the finding rather than weakening it: the message never prints the `nvm install` line from `.nvmrc` (which holds `24`) that §11 promises, and `.npmrc` has `engine-strict=true` with `engines.node: ">=24.16"`, which gates dependency installs but does not gate `npm run start`, so the `npm start` route inherits the same crash. The candidate's file, line and mechanism are all correct as stated.
